          %p There are two different meanings of phrase “matrix factorization” which are closely related
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                (typically canonical)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matrix+decomposition"} matrix decompositions
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matrices"} matrices
                into
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matrix+product"} products of matrices
                of specific kind, like
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gauss+decomposition"} Gauss decomposition
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polar+decomposition"} polar decomposition
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QR+decomposition"} QR decomposition
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/LU+decomposition"} LU decomposition
                etc.
            %li
              %p
                decomposition of an element of a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+matrix"} identity matrix
          %p This entry is dedicated to the latter as it concerns appearance of certain categories in mathematical physics.
          %h2#overview Overview
          %p
            Matrix factorizations were introduced by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Eisenbud"} David Eisenbud
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+algebra"} commutative algebra
          %p
            Matrix factorizations arise in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            as categories of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-branes"} D-branes
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Landau-Ginzburg+model"} Landau-Ginzburg
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-model"} B-model
          %p
            Matrix factorizations have also been used by Khovanov-Rozansky in knot theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0401268"} math/0401268
          %p
            %strong Matrix factorization categories
            are examples of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+categories"} Calabi-Yau categories
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TCFT"} TCFT
          %p
            Dyckerhoff has proved that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hochschild+homology"} Hochschild homology
            of the matrix factorizations category of an isolated singularity is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacobian+ring"} Jacobian ring
            of the singularity. See also the work of E. Segal and Caldararu-Tu. In light of the Costello/Kontsevich/Hopkins-Lurie theorem, this result has been anticipated for some time, as the closed state space of a Landau-Ginzburg B-model is the Jacobian ring.
          %p There is also the Calabi-Yau/Landau-Ginzburg correspondence. In some cases, categories of matrix factorizations turn out to be equivalent to categories of coherent sheaves.
          %p For general theory and properties of matrix factorizations, see work of Orlov. For example, matrix factorization categories are related to derived categories of singularities.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            (Eisenbud 1980) A
            %strong matrix factorization
            of an element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            in a commutative ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is an ordered pair of maps of free
            = succeed "-modules" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %mi F
                  %mo →
                  %mi G
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo :
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\phi:F\to G,\psi: G\to F)
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo =
                  %mi x
                  %mo ⋅
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi\circ\psi = x\cdot 1_G
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo =
                    %mi x
                    %mo ⋅
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi\circ\phi = x\cdot 1_F
          %p
            Note that if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\phi,\psi)
            is a matrix factorization of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            annihilates
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Coker
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Coker\phi
            = succeed "-modules" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo ,
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F,G
            are free, one assumes that they are finitely generated projective.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Landau-Ginzburg+model"} Landau-Ginzburg model
